---
<p>No exceptions: a failing function says <code>fails</code> in its signature, and the error is a value from a named set. Callers propagate with <code>try</code> or handle with <code>catch</code>. <code>panic</code> is for bugs like an index out of range and aborts instead of unwinding.</p>

<h2 id="handle">Return and handle an error</h2>
<p><code>fail .x</code> leaves the function with an error; <code>return</code> always returns a value. <code>try f()</code> passes the error to your caller, which needs a matching <code>fails</code>. <code>f() catch value</code> supplies a fallback; <code>f() catch switch { … }</code> handles each case and must list them all.</p>
<example name="cookbook-errors-try"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Go">n, err := parsePort(text)
if err != nil { return 0, err }</code></pre>
<p>Ignoring a failing call is a compile error; an error never passes a function that does not declare it.</p>

<h2 id="custom">Define your own error type</h2>
<p><code>union error</code> declares cases, each able to carry data. Cases are named by set (<code>ConfigError.missing</code>); a <code>catch switch</code> arm binds the payload with <code>let</code>. Sets compose (<code>union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError</code>) or narrow with <code>except</code> (spec §3.14).</p>
<example name="cookbook-errors"/>

<h2 id="effects">Effects in signatures</h2>
<p>A signature also says what the function touches. A public function that reads a file declares <code>uses fs, alloc fails IoError</code>; leaving <code>uses</code> out stops the compiler with BYN6002, which suggests the missing effects, so callers see it reads files.</p>
<example name="cookbook-effects"/>

<h2 id="defer">Clean up when a scope ends</h2>
<p><code>defer stmt</code> runs when the scope exits, however it exits; deferred statements run in reverse order. <code>defer failing stmt</code> runs only when the function fails, for a rollback; <code>defer panicking</code> only during a panic. Deferred bodies cannot fail. A <code>unique</code> value is cleaned up when its single owner goes away, so there is no <code>using</code> for disposal (spec §7.6).</p>
<example name="cookbook-defer"/>
<pre><code class="other" data-lang="Python">try:
    work()
finally:
    close()</code></pre>
